course: dictionary
lesson: 1471
title: Catapult in context
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Catapult can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action). Read the sentence around it to find out which meaning you need. «Attackers besieged castles for weeks with catapults and trebuchets» uses catapults as things, big war machines that threw heavy stones.
explain: In the UK, a catapult is also a small Y-shaped stick with a rubber band, used to shoot little stones. In the USA this is called a slingshot. The word is the same, but the place changes what it means in everyday life.
explain: As a verb, to catapult means to throw or launch something hard and fast. «A chameleon fires its tongue like a catapult» shows the speed: the tongue shoots out in a split second. Test the meaning by swapping in "shoot out fast" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «He was catapulted to fame» means he was suddenly moved into a famous situation, very fast. This is a phrase, so the meaning is not about throwing at all. When a word seems strange, check whether it is part of a phrase.
fact: Catapult is a noun (a catapult, catapults) and a verb (catapult, catapulted, catapulting).
fact: «Catapult» can mean an old war machine that throws heavy stones or objects a long way.
fact: In the UK, a catapult is a Y-shaped stick with a rubber band; in the USA it is called a slingshot.
fact: «Catapulted» means thrown or launched hard and fast, or suddenly moved into a new situation.
fact: Scientists use the idea of a catapult to test forces and energy, and fleas use it too: a cat flea stores energy in a rubbery pad and lets go all at once.
exercise: «A chameleon fires its tongue like a catapult and grabs a cricket in a split second.» What does catapult mean here? | *Shoot out quickly, like a launch | Stay very still for a long time | Hide slowly under a leaf | Climb a tree to get away
exercise: «Attackers besieged castles for weeks with catapults and trebuchets.» What is a catapult in this sentence? | A small rubber band for holding hair | A toy car with wheels | A type of castle wall | *An old war machine that throws heavy stones a long way
exercise: «The young singer was catapulted to fame after one video.» What does catapulted to fame mean? | Thrown into a river by fans | Asked to leave the stage | Slowly walked to a famous place | *Suddenly moved into being famous
test: In the UK, a Y-shaped stick with a rubber band is a catapult. What is it called in the USA? | Catapault | Sling | Stone-thrower | *Slingshot
test: «A cat flea can jump about 30 centimetres by letting its energy go all at once like a catapult.» What does like a catapult suggest? | Upside down, on the wall | Without any energy at all | Very slowly and gently | *Suddenly, with great force
exam: Which meaning fits catapult as a verb? | *Throw or launch something hard and fast | Stay very still and quiet | Sleep for a long time after lunch | Build a castle wall around a hill
